% ============================================================================
% muscle-body-aspect — the anterior/posterior/ventral SPATIAL ASPECT a named
% muscle sits on, decoded from clauses already sitting unused inside
% `muscle-groups.adj`'s own already-quoted Wikipedia source sentences
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `muscle-groups.adj`
% (`muscle_region(muscle, region)`, biceps_brachii/triceps_brachii →
% arm, rectus_abdominis → abdomen, sartorius/quadriceps → thigh,
% gastrocnemius → leg): that table's own header already quotes, verbatim,
% Wikipedia sentences that name each muscle's body REGION -- but three of
% those same quoted spans also name the muscle's spatial ASPECT within that
% region (anterior/posterior compartment, or ventral aspect), a fact the
% muscle/region schema had no room for:
%
%   rectus_abdominis → ventral
%     "The rectus abdominis ... is a pair of segmented skeletal muscle on the
%      ventral aspect of a person's abdomen."
%
%   sartorius → anterior
%     "It is a long, thin, superficial muscle that runs down the length of
%      the thigh in the anterior compartment."
%
%   gastrocnemius → posterior
%     "It is located superficial to the soleus in the posterior (back)
%      compartment of the leg."
%
% New `muscle_body_aspect(muscle, aspect)` table decodes those three spans as
% their own rows. IMPORTANT SCOPE NOTE: the other six muscles in
% `muscle-groups.adj` (biceps_brachii, triceps_brachii, deltoid,
% pectoralis_major, gluteus_maximus, quadriceps) are deliberately NOT rowed
% here. Their own quoted spans use everyday words like "front"/"back" of a
% limb ("lies on the front of the upper arm", "a large muscle on the back of
% the upper limb", "muscles on the front of the thigh") rather than the
% anatomical terms "anterior"/"posterior"/"ventral"/"compartment"/"aspect"
% this table's atoms are built from -- per this stdlib's own established
% precedent (`muscle-groups.adj`'s own header: "Each `region` value is a
% SINGLE lowercase token that appears VERBATIM in the cited source
% sentence"), an atom must echo the source's OWN chosen word, not a
% same-meaning paraphrase, so those six are honest abstentions rather than a
% guessed anterior/posterior label the source's own sentence never states.
%
% "Aspect" (the muscle's spatial position -- anterior, posterior, or
% ventral) is categorically distinct from the parent table's "region" (which
% limb or body area the muscle sits in, e.g. thigh or leg) -- the sartorius
% and quadriceps share the SAME region (thigh), but only the sartorius's own
% quote names an aspect. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? muscle_body_aspect(sartorius, $Aspect)  % anterior
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `muscle_body_aspect(muscle, aspect)` carrying the citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the aspect
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of these three muscles (it never invents an
% aspect). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind an aspect and recall the
% muscle that sits there:
%
%     ? muscle_body_aspect($M, posterior)  % gastrocnemius
%
% A recall on `quadriceps` -- a real, already-tabled muscle in
% `muscle-groups.adj`, sharing the thigh region with sartorius, but whose own
% quoted span ("muscles on the front of the thigh") never uses the word
% "anterior" -- correctly abstains rather than inventing an aspect for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, spans already quoted inside
% `muscle-groups.adj`'s own header, from the SAME English Wikipedia articles
% that table already cites -- no new WebFetch, only a different schema
% decoding a different clause of each already-verified quote. `trust
% consensus` -- the same tier the parent table already carries (Wikipedia is
% a secondary reference, not a primary government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table muscle_body_aspect {
    columns muscle, aspect

    row (rectus_abdominis, ventral)
    row (sartorius, anterior)
    row (gastrocnemius, posterior)

    source "The rectus abdominis ... is a pair of segmented skeletal muscle on the ventral aspect of a person's abdomen."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rectus_abdominis_muscle"
    trust consensus
}
